# gen_fu_l53_outstanding_saturate.log -- the outstanding counters saturate, and three properties stop
# claiming to be DUT obligations (TB Infra, 2026-09-05).
#
# THE ONE BUILD EVERY FIGURE COMES FROM
# $ bash dv/auto_dv/tb/gen_tb_local.sh compile <root>/dv/auto_dv/out_l53b     compile rc=0
#
# ================ THE DEFECT, FOUND BY rtl-arch FROM TWO RUN LOGS ================
# gen_protocol_props.sv:122 declares both counters `int unsigned`, and :128-129 updated them as
# `counter + (req & gnt) - rvalid`. A response arriving with NOTHING OUTSTANDING computes 0 + 0 - 1 and WRAPS
# to 4294967295 rather than saturating. rtl-arch read it out of two run logs without a wave: in one seed the
# bound property first fires exactly one cycle after the first response-without-outstanding, the counter's own
# update delay, then floods; in another the response obligation fails 70199 times while the bound stays silent
# for eleven million ticks, which is the same-cycle case this file's own comment already names.
#
# ================ TEN READERS, NOT THREE, AND THE THIRD CONSEQUENCE IS THE QUIET ONE ================
# rtl-arch found a third DUT-annotated property I had missed (:220, the data no-third-request obligation).
# Sweeping the file properly then found TEN readers of these two counters: five assertions and FIVE COVERS.
#   assertions :184 :185 :208 :209 :220      covers :189 :215 :216 :225 :226
# Every cover tests a counter for an EXACT value (1, 2, or the maximum). Once a counter reads four billion
# those are false for the rest of the run and the covers SILENTLY STOP BEING HIT. No assertion fires for that.
# THREE CLASSES OF CONSEQUENCE: bound assertions that fire; a request-rule assertion that ACCUSES THE CORE of
# a rule it never broke; and covers that go dark without a word. The third is the quietest and the worst.
# IT ALSO DECIDES SATURATE-VERSUS-QUALIFY, which neither of us had argued: qualifying the assertions leaves
# the COVERS broken, because their problem is the counter's VALUE and not the properties' terms. Counter
# first, annotations after.
#
# ================ THE A/B, BUILT BOTH WAYS SO ONLY THE COUNTER DIFFERS ================
# MUTATION MUT-SPURIOUSRVALID in the bus driver: a one-shot response asserted at a fixed cycle with the
# pending queue empty, i.e. a response with nothing outstanding. Two builds, identical but for the counter.
#                             dbus response obligation      dbus bound property
#   wrapping + mutation              3 fires                  5285 error lines
#   saturating + mutation            3 fires                       0
# THE CLAIM THAT DECIDES THE DESIGN IS MEASURED, not argued: the response obligation fires THE SAME THREE
# TIMES in both, so the real event is still reported and saturating loses NO report. That is the whole reason
# saturation beats qualification, and it now rests on a measurement rather than on my reading of the two
# properties' firing sets.
#
# ================ TWO CONSEQUENCES THIS FIXTURE DOES **NOT** DEMONSTRATE ================
# Stated because I asserted both earlier and the fixture does not support either:
#   - the no-third-request ACCUSATION: 0 fires in BOTH builds, even with the counter wrapped, because the
#     program makes no data request during the wrapped window. rtl-arch's reasoning stands and is UNMEASURED.
#   - the COVER DEATH: those covers read 0 match in BOTH builds, so this fixture never exercises them and
#     nothing is shown either way.
# Showing both needs a program that makes data requests while wrapped and reaches the depths those covers
# test, which is stimulus work larger than this touch and is not folded into it.
# TWO MEASUREMENT ERRORS OF MY OWN, corrected before they reached this log: my first cover check counted
# assertion SUMMARY LINES rather than match counts and read as "3 hit in both"; and a combined grep hid that
# the mutation landed on the DATA bus, not the instruction bus (every ibus property reads 0 throughout).
#
# ================ THE EXPOSURE IS REAL AND UNREALISED, WHICH SETS THE PRIORITY ================
# rtl-arch bounded it rather than leaving it open: ZERO of the 53 runs in the measured round fired any
# counter-bound property, and zero in the rebaseline. No measured round has ever wrapped a counter. So this
# matters for future rounds and NOTHING ALREADY RECORDED needs revisiting - which is why it did not jump ahead
# of the irq checker work.
#
# ================ THE FIX ================
# BOTH COUNTERS SATURATE at zero. The decrement is at most one, so saturation is exactly "a response with
# nothing outstanding leaves the counter at zero", and the five covers then read true values again.
# THE THREE ANNOTATIONS MOVE TO TB, each stating the DUT obligation it observes so the obligation is not lost
# with the label: never more than eight instruction beats in flight; never more than two data beats, the two
# halves of one split; and a request is never issued with two outstanding. The ruling was that a label is read
# downstream as "a failure here is a design bug", and a property whose bound term is this TB's own model
# cannot carry that reading saturated or not, since a wrong model fires it with no DUT behaviour at all.
# I did not make that call alone: I put both framings to the Orchestrator and rtl-arch rather than relabel
# three design obligations on my own reading of what a label is for.
# $ after the change, no DUT-annotated assertion reads either counter: 0
#
# ================ ALSO IN THIS TOUCH: THE DEBUG-TERM HYGIENE ================
# The classifier's pre-state override now reads (st.is_intr && !st.debug_mode). The Critic proved a debug
# entry cannot carry rvfi_intr, so this changes no behaviour; it carries the term the RTL's own guard carries
# (the clear at ibex_cs_registers.sv:924 sits inside else if (!debug_mode_i) at :918) so the classifier does
# not depend on a reachability argument staying true.
#
# ================ GATES AND SMOKES ================
# $ four smokes on the build above, verdicts from each run's own verdict.txt, referee counts from sim.log
#   storm PASS ref 0 | lines PASS ref 0 | dbgstorm PASS ref 0 | fetchen PASS ref 0
# $ compile rc=0; no DUT-annotated assertion reads either counter (measured, 0)
# The counter finding's register row is the DV Lead's to place; it has the finding rather than my wording.
# END. Every figure above is produced by the command printed above its block.
